The scale of a spectral axis that is described by a grism dispersion function (the -GRI and -GRA algorithms of FITS-WCS paper III) is now found analytically when a FITS header is read and when one is written. It was found by numerical differentiation, which is good to about one part in 1e9. Positions far from the reference pixel of a header that was read were in error by about 1e-6 pixels, and the CDELT value that was written was in error by about 1.5 parts in 1e10. For the examples given in the paper the Mappings now agree with WCSLIB 8.6 to about 1e-11 pixels.
